slot-0 read, since an uninitialized slot does not match ^test either and R8's polarity alone passes against the bug; R9 (`our $g = "test"; $g =~ /^test/ ? 1 : 0` -> Int:1, the OPf_STACKED form). Unblocks t/base/pat.t, which compiles AS ITSELF and matches perl on stdout and exit status: tbase floor 2 -> 3, executable-gate floor 6 -> 7. gate-green 161->164; gap-declared unchanged at 21. Prior: perl5-son 2162f8e: a back-edge-less `enterloop` (nextop == lastop) is a lexical scope, not a loop. Every enterloop -- the opcode pair a bare `{ ... }` block AND a block-form `package`/`class Foo { ... }` declaration BOTH compile to -- was routed into the while-loop translator regardless of a back-edge; `_walk_loop_body` then accepted an in-block statement-modifier `and`/`or` as the loop's HEADER CONDITION, hoisting it to the top and dropping every statement after it as unreachable loop "body" -- a SILENT DROP with no GAP raised (`my $c=0; { print "first\n" if $c; print "after\n"; } print "end\n";` printed only "end", dropping "after") -- or, on the polarity where the guard is true, built a malformed loop graph that made `lli` die on SIGNAL 11. FIX (producer, perl5-son SoN/FromOptree.pm, `_translate_while_loop` call site): discriminate on `nextop == lastop` (both `leaveloop`, the same back-edge check `_is_postfix_while` already uses) and walk a back-edge-less enterloop's body INLINE as a lexical scope instead of dispatching it to the loop translator; the body then terminates on `leaveloop`, which the main walk already handles. Four new GREEN corpus cases, ALL EXECUTING (none GAP-declared -- a GAP-declared case is `pass()`ed without running its source and would pin nothing): control-flow.md T10 (`{ my $z = 5; print "b\n"; }` -> stdout b + Bool:1, a plain bare block, no branching); T11 (`package Foo { print "in\n" }` -> stdout in + Bool:1, the identical enterloop shape a block-form `package`/`class` declaration produces -- 159 of this project's own 166 lib/ files contain it); T12/T13 the bilateral pair proving BOTH failure modes closed over the identical source with only the guard's value flipped: T12 `my $c=0; { print "first\n" if $c; print "after\n"; } print "end\n"; 1` -> stdout after\nend\n + Int:1 (was: silent drop, printed only "end"); T13 `my $c=1; { same }` -> stdout first\nafter\nend\n + Int:1 (was: lli signal 11). gate-green 157->161; gap-declared unchanged at 21. Prior: perl5-son R1.0 effect-by-default for direct/method Calls: FromOptree::_handle_entersub only pinned a Call's control_in `if $void`, so a NON-void call (its result bound/used, e.g. `my $r = f()`) got NO control pin at all -- unreachable from Return, it was either dropped entirely by the producer's reachability walk (F4: `sub f { print "x"; 2 } my $r = f(); 3` printed nothing, not "x") or, when reachable via its value use, could float past a FOLLOWING effect and reorder it (F3: `sub f { print "c\n"; 2 } print "a\n"; my $r = f(); print "b\n"; $r;` printed a/b/c instead of a/c/b). FIX (producer, perl5-son SoN/FromOptree.pm _handle_entersub, both the direct-call and method-dispatch call sites): pin control_in unconditionally (void or not), advance the sim's control to the Call, and push its value only when non-void -- mirroring the existing void-print precedent. _place_call, now dead, is deleted. Corpus subs.md F13 (`sub f { print "x"; 2 } my $r = f(); 3` -> stdout x + Int:3, the unused-result case: like F9 but the call's OWN value is never read, only its effect matters). gate-green 156->157; gap-declared unchanged. Prior: Slice B Commit 2-print: _lower_call_direct now threads a callee's STRAIGHT-LINE Print-only control chain (Print -> ... -> Start, no Region/Loop/If/Assign) in program order instead of GAPping -- _collect_straight_line_effect_chain walks control_in collecting Print nodes (GAPping loudly on anything else, preserving Commit 1's guard for every other effect shape), and _lower_inlined_effects_then_value drives that chain through process_control_node inside the arg-bind frame BEFORE lowering the Return value, splicing the callee's prints onto the caller's control chain in program order. The load-bearing hazard: lower_value's cache is keyed by the node's STRING id, and each graph numbers nodes from its own factory, so a callee Print#N can collide with an unrelated caller Print#N and be served the caller's cached ref (silently re-dropping the callee's effect -- the exact bug this closes). Fixed by swapping the WHOLE cache to a fresh hash for the duration of the chain drive (mirrors the existing refaddr-keyed _arg_bind frame pattern) -- this also makes two-call-site inlining of the same effectful callee correct for free (each inline gets its own fresh cache, so the callee's print re-emits at both sites, matching perl running the sub body twice). Three new GREEN corpus cases: subs.md F9 (`sub f { print "x"; 2 } my $r = f(); $r` -> stdout x + Int:2, the ordered-print case, previously GAP-declared under Commit 1); F10 (caller print + inlined callee print, `stdout: callercallee`, the cache-collision proof -- caller and callee Print nodes DO collide by id in the real B::SoN-loaded graph, measured); F11 (the same effectful callee inlined at two call sites, `stdout: yy`, the two-call-site proof). One new GAP-declared case: F12 (a loop-effect callee -- Region on the control chain -- still GAPs cleanly, the parked loop-store shape, Commit 2-loop-store, is a separate slice). gate-green 153->156; gap-declared unchanged at 19 (F9 flipped GAP->GREEN, F12 added GAP). Prior: Slice B Commit 1: _lower_call_direct now GAPs loudly (does not silently drop the effect) when an inlined callee's Return.control_in is neither undef nor Start -- an effectful callee (print/store/loop on its control chain) previously had that effect silently dropped at inline time. GAP-declared cases never touch gate_green (they are corpus-skipped before the triple-contract legs run), so the floor is unchanged at 153; gap-declared count rises 18->19. Prior: Coerce(Int-